1
00:00:00,109 --> 00:00:02,783
A Rubik's cube looks like a puzzle about colors.

2
00:00:03,125 --> 00:00:05,269
But watch what happens when a face turns.

3
00:00:05,598 --> 00:00:09,930
Each little piece travels through space, and its stickers turn with it.

4
00:00:10,258 --> 00:00:13,381
What if our program remembered that geometry directly?

5
00:00:13,723 --> 00:00:15,854
This is the idea behind Rubix.

6
00:00:17,330 --> 00:00:19,678
Follow just this green and white edge.

7
00:00:20,006 --> 00:00:22,749
To describe it, we need to answer two questions.

8
00:00:23,091 --> 00:00:24,067
Where is the piece?

9
00:00:24,395 --> 00:00:26,336
And which way do its two stickers point?

10
00:00:26,664 --> 00:00:30,520
Rubix answers both questions with the same rotation matrix.

11
00:00:30,849 --> 00:00:34,732
To see why that works, let's first give the cube a coordinate system.

12
00:00:36,164 --> 00:00:38,458
Place the origin at the middle of the cube.

13
00:00:38,786 --> 00:00:46,148
In Rubix, positive x points toward the green center, positive y toward red, and positive z toward white.

14
00:00:46,490 --> 00:00:49,966
Their opposite directions are blue, orange, and yellow.

15
00:00:50,294 --> 00:00:51,732
We keep this frame fixed.

16
00:00:52,156 --> 00:00:57,126
Face turns spin the centers in place, but do not move them to other faces.

17
00:00:58,525 --> 00:01:01,770
Now replace every little piece by a point at its center.

18
00:01:02,099 --> 00:01:08,836
Along each axis, there are only three possible coordinates: minus one, zero, and one.

19
00:01:09,178 --> 00:01:11,933
This gives a three by three by three lattice.

20
00:01:12,275 --> 00:01:16,009
The point at the origin has no stickers, so Rubix leaves it out.

21
00:01:16,338 --> 00:01:17,844
Twenty six points remain.

22
00:01:19,220 --> 00:01:24,231
A nonzero coordinate means the piece reaches an outer face along that axis.

23
00:01:24,560 --> 00:01:28,049
Count the nonzero coordinates, and you count its stickers.

24
00:01:28,378 --> 00:01:29,354
One gives a center.

25
00:01:29,696 --> 00:01:30,712
Two gives an edge.

26
00:01:31,082 --> 00:01:32,098
Three gives a corner.

27
00:01:32,427 --> 00:01:38,525
The geometry already contains the information we might otherwise encode as a separate piece type.

28
00:01:40,010 --> 00:01:45,551
For our green and white edge, the solved coordinate is one, zero, one.

29
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:46,991
Call this vector c.

30
00:01:47,347 --> 00:01:52,290
Here comes an important distinction: c is a permanent name for this particular piece.

31
00:01:52,619 --> 00:01:55,334
Even after a scramble, c never changes.

32
00:01:55,662 --> 00:01:59,532
It says where the piece belongs, rather than where it happens to be now.

33
00:02:00,956 --> 00:02:03,902
Beside c, store a rotation matrix R.

34
00:02:04,393 --> 00:02:11,198
Think of its columns as three arrows: where the original x, y, and z directions have landed.

35
00:02:11,513 --> 00:02:14,472
A matrix is a description of a transformation.

36
00:02:14,814 --> 00:02:20,356
Once we know what it does to these three basis vectors, we know what it does to every vector.

37
00:02:21,775 --> 00:02:24,300
In particular, we know what it does to c.

38
00:02:24,751 --> 00:02:28,648
Multiplying R by c gives the piece's current position, p.

39
00:02:28,976 --> 00:02:32,846
At the start, R is the identity, so p equals c.

40
00:02:33,175 --> 00:02:36,814
After a turn, R carries the home position to a new point.

41
00:02:37,156 --> 00:02:41,216
We do not need to store a second, independently updated position.

42
00:02:42,622 --> 00:02:44,821
Take this top face move from Rubix.

43
00:02:45,149 --> 00:02:50,038
It leaves z alone, sends x to minus y, and sends y to x.

44
00:02:50,367 --> 00:02:55,745
Our edge travels from one, zero, one to zero, minus one, one.

45
00:02:56,087 --> 00:02:58,693
The continuous arc is only an animation.

46
00:02:59,035 --> 00:03:03,082
The program stores the exact integer result of the quarter turn.

47
00:03:04,484 --> 00:03:06,615
Look at the columns of the move matrix.

48
00:03:06,957 --> 00:03:12,498
The first is zero, minus one, zero: the destination of the x arrow.

49
00:03:12,840 --> 00:03:18,028
The second is one, zero, zero: the destination of the y arrow.

50
00:03:18,370 --> 00:03:20,799
The third keeps the z arrow fixed.

51
00:03:21,128 --> 00:03:26,696
Matrix multiplication combines these destinations using the coordinates of our vector.

52
00:03:28,109 --> 00:03:29,193
Now for the stickers.

53
00:03:29,522 --> 00:03:35,022
Represent a sticker by its outward normal: an arrow perpendicular to its surface.

54
00:03:35,351 --> 00:03:40,960
Green's permanent color identity is positive x, its direction in the solved cube.

55
00:03:41,288 --> 00:03:45,443
After our top turn, that same green sticker points toward negative y.

56
00:03:45,785 --> 00:03:49,601
Its identity stays green; its current direction changes.

57
00:03:51,039 --> 00:03:54,365
Why does the home coordinate tell us which stickers the piece owns?

58
00:03:54,694 --> 00:03:57,599
Split c into its three axis components.

59
00:03:57,928 --> 00:04:02,857
Our edge gives one x arrow, no y arrow, and one z arrow.

60
00:04:03,186 --> 00:04:06,702
These are exactly its green and white sticker normals at home.

61
00:04:07,031 --> 00:04:11,648
Negative components work too: minus x identifies a blue sticker.

62
00:04:13,067 --> 00:04:17,589
Rather than adding the component arrows, put them side by side as columns.

63
00:04:17,918 --> 00:04:21,448
That is the diagonal matrix of c, which we'll call D.

64
00:04:21,817 --> 00:04:25,646
For this edge, its diagonal is one, zero, one.

65
00:04:25,975 --> 00:04:28,826
The zero column represents an absent sticker.

66
00:04:29,195 --> 00:04:31,733
For a corner all three columns are present.

67
00:04:32,089 --> 00:04:33,676
For a center only one is.

68
00:04:35,067 --> 00:04:37,932
Multiplication acts on each column separately.

69
00:04:38,260 --> 00:04:41,790
So R times D rotates all of the sticker normals at once.

70
00:04:42,132 --> 00:04:44,970
The green column now points along negative y.

71
00:04:45,312 --> 00:04:46,994
The white column still points up.

72
00:04:47,336 --> 00:04:49,195
The zero column stays zero.

73
00:04:49,537 --> 00:04:54,521
The very same R that moved the piece also describes where every sticker faces.

74
00:04:55,956 --> 00:04:58,698
There is an especially satisfying connection here.

75
00:04:59,026 --> 00:05:01,429
Add the columns of D and you get c.

76
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:06,266
Rotate them and add again, and you get R c, the current position.

77
00:05:06,594 --> 00:05:10,682
So the transformed sticker normals also determine where the piece sits.

78
00:05:11,010 --> 00:05:15,695
Location and orientation are two views of one geometric description.

79
00:05:17,109 --> 00:05:19,566
A face turn should affect only one layer.

80
00:05:19,894 --> 00:05:22,052
Let v point outward from that face.

81
00:05:22,394 --> 00:05:27,188
The dot product v dot p measures the piece's coordinate along that direction.

82
00:05:27,516 --> 00:05:29,810
Select the pieces for which it is positive.

83
00:05:30,166 --> 00:05:33,193
For the top face, that means z equals one.

84
00:05:33,522 --> 00:05:35,897
Exactly nine pieces pass the test.

85
00:05:37,331 --> 00:05:40,358
The test must use the current position, R c.

86
00:05:40,687 --> 00:05:46,964
After our top turn, the green and white edge lives on the left face, even though its home coordinate was on

87
00:05:46,964 --> 00:05:47,614
the front.

88
00:05:47,942 --> 00:05:50,521
Selecting by c would move the wrong pieces.

89
00:05:50,850 --> 00:05:55,345
Selecting by R c automatically follows every piece through the scramble.

90
00:05:56,774 --> 00:06:00,142
For each selected piece, update R to M times R.

91
00:06:00,484 --> 00:06:05,019
The old R acts first, taking the piece from home to its current state.

92
00:06:05,361 --> 00:06:07,180
The new move M acts next.

93
00:06:07,522 --> 00:06:10,060
That order explains why M goes on the left.

94
00:06:10,389 --> 00:06:15,740
Matrix multiplication becomes the record of a piece's entire history of turns.

95
00:06:17,150 --> 00:06:20,830
And here is the whole update in the language of the implementation.

96
00:06:21,158 --> 00:06:22,759
Compute the current position.

97
00:06:23,088 --> 00:06:26,007
Use a dot product to test whether the move applies.

98
00:06:26,335 --> 00:06:30,124
If it does, compose the move matrix with the stored rotation.

99
00:06:30,452 --> 00:06:32,067
Otherwise keep that rotation.

100
00:06:32,395 --> 00:06:36,319
Repeat this rule over the twenty six pairs that make up the cube.

101
00:06:37,734 --> 00:06:42,541
The animation passes through arbitrary angles, but stored states never do.

102
00:06:42,883 --> 00:06:47,432
Quarter turns only permute the coordinate axes and reverse some signs.

103
00:06:47,774 --> 00:06:51,807
Their matrices have entries minus one, zero, and one.

104
00:06:52,149 --> 00:06:55,231
They stay orthogonal, with determinant plus one.

105
00:06:55,573 --> 00:06:58,873
There are just twenty four such proper rotations of a cube.

106
00:06:59,201 --> 00:07:02,378
Exact endpoints avoid accumulated rounding error.

107
00:07:03,789 --> 00:07:07,034
This gives us useful checks as well as an elegant model.

108
00:07:07,389 --> 00:07:11,300
Four quarter turns return every piece and sticker to the start.

109
00:07:11,629 --> 00:07:14,045
A move followed by its inverse cancels.

110
00:07:14,373 --> 00:07:21,083
These identities test the geometry as a whole, rather than checking a few hand written sticker permutations.

111
00:07:22,484 --> 00:07:23,650
When is a piece solved?

112
00:07:23,992 --> 00:07:27,509
When every sticker normal points in its original direction.

113
00:07:27,837 --> 00:07:32,631
That is precisely R times the diagonal of c equals the diagonal of c.

114
00:07:32,973 --> 00:07:38,596
Because position is the sum of those columns, this equality also puts the piece at home.

115
00:07:38,924 --> 00:07:42,033
Rubix checks this condition for every piece.

116
00:07:43,456 --> 00:07:45,940
Why not simply demand that R be the identity?

117
00:07:46,268 --> 00:07:48,385
Watch the white center during a top turn.

118
00:07:48,714 --> 00:07:53,956
Its little local x and y arrows rotate, but its only sticker still points up.

119
00:07:54,325 --> 00:07:57,203
For an ordinary cube, that center is solved.

120
00:07:57,532 --> 00:08:03,317
Rubix correctly ignores the orientation of an unmarked center within its own face.

121
00:08:03,646 --> 00:08:05,967
A picture cube would need a stricter condition.

122
00:08:07,400 --> 00:08:11,012
We now have a complete description of the puzzle's state and motion.

123
00:08:11,341 --> 00:08:13,743
A home vector identifies the piece.

124
00:08:14,072 --> 00:08:17,534
A rotation gives its position and sticker directions.

125
00:08:17,862 --> 00:08:19,789
A dot product chooses a layer.

126
00:08:20,118 --> 00:08:22,181
Multiplication applies the move.

127
00:08:22,509 --> 00:08:25,183
And an equality recognizes solved pieces.

128
00:08:25,525 --> 00:08:32,086
The search for a solution is a separate problem; this encoding supplies its consistent geometric world.

129
00:08:33,484 --> 00:08:40,019
If this way of seeing matrices appeals to you, watch Grant Sanderson's Essence of Linear Algebra, from Three

130
00:08:40,019 --> 00:08:41,090
Blue One Brown.

131
00:08:41,418 --> 00:08:47,884
It is a superb series: patient, visual, and beautifully clear about why the mathematics works.

132
00:08:48,212 --> 00:08:50,248
It directly inspired Rubix.

133
00:08:50,576 --> 00:08:54,106
This original explanation follows that geometric spirit.

134
00:08:54,448 --> 00:09:02,109
Once you see a matrix as motion, a cube of colored stickers becomes a small world of vectors and transformations.
